# ══ MINIMUM FOR LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ═════════════════════════════════════════════
# Five values are mandatory. The server exits at boot without TO_EMAIL, and
# rejects every form submission without both Turnstile keys — so a .env missing
# any of them fails immediately rather than half-working.
#
# The fastest way to a running local site is to skip this template entirely and
# write the six lines below straight into server/.env, from the repo root:
#
#     cat > server/.env <<'ENVEOF'
#     TO_EMAIL=dev@example.com
#     GMAIL_USER=dev@example.com
#     GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=not-a-real-password
#     TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=1x00000000000000000000AA
#     TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#     PORT=8123
#     ENVEOF
#
# (In PowerShell, create the file in an editor instead — the heredoc is bash.)
#
# That gives you the whole site working locally: pages, PDF downloads, both
# rate-limit layers, CSRF, and Turnstile. The two Turnstile values are
# Cloudflare's published always-pass test keys, documented further down. Mail
# delivery is the one thing that fails, at the final step — which is usually
# what you want while developing, since it cannot reach the company inbox.
#
# Never use the values above in production: the always-pass secret means every
# bot clears verification. Use the template below instead, and see the
# production section at the end of this file.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# Gmail account used only to send mail — create a dedicated account for this,
# then generate an "app password" at myaccount.google.com > Security > App passwords.
# Do NOT use your personal Gmail password here.
GMAIL_USER=your-sending-account@gmail.com          # (required)
GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx             # (required)

# Where enquiries land. The server refuses to start without this one.
TO_EMAIL=info@sblenergy.com                        # (required)

# Optional: send job applications to a different inbox (e.g. HR). Falls back to TO_EMAIL if unset.
CAREER_EMAIL=

PORT=8123

# ── Cloudflare Turnstile ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both keys come from the SAME widget in the Cloudflare dashboard (a test sitekey
# needs its matching test secret). Without them the contact and career forms
# reject every submission. In production the widget must also list your real
# hostnames — including the www variant, which is the usual thing to miss.
#
# For local development use Cloudflare's published test keys below rather than
# your real ones: real keys are locked to the hostnames on the widget, so they
# refuse to render on localhost. Pick one row and use BOTH of its values — a test
# sitekey paired with a real secret fails verification in a confusing way.
# Reference: https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/troubleshooting/testing/
#
#   always passes
#     TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=1x00000000000000000000AA
#     TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#
#   always blocks — use this to check that a rejected submission leaves the form
#   usable, rather than forcing the visitor to reload the page
#     TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=2x00000000000000000000AB
#     TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=2x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#
#   forces an interactive challenge (pair with the always-passes secret)
#     TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=3x00000000000000000000FF
#
#   reports "token already spent" (pair with the always-passes sitekey)
#     TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=3x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#
# Swap in the real keys before going live. The always-passes secret means every
# bot clears verification, so shipping it disables the protection entirely.
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Production settings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave both blank for local development.

# Set to "production" when deploying. This is what puts the Secure flag on the
# CSRF and session cookies, so without it they can travel over plain HTTP.
NODE_ENV=

# Signs the visitor session cookie that per-visitor rate limits are keyed on.
# Leave blank and the server invents one at each boot, which is fine locally.
# In production set it so sessions survive restarts, and keep it secret —
# anyone holding it can mint session ids. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
SESSION_SECRET=

# Who is allowed to tell this server the visitor's IP address, via the
# X-Forwarded-For header. Blank means "nobody" — the header is ignored and the
# real connection address is used, which is correct for local development.
#
# In production set it to match whatever sits in front of Node:
#   loopback              nginx/Caddy on the same machine (the common case)
#   1                     exactly one proxy in front, whoever it happens to be
#   10.0.0.5,1.2.3.0/24   specific proxy addresses or ranges
#
# Never set this to "true" — that trusts the header the visitor wrote themselves,
# which lets anyone forge an IP and slip past the rate limits. The server refuses
# that value and falls back to blank.
#
# Getting it wrong in the strict direction is safe: every visitor appears to share
# the proxy's address, so the per-IP limits apply to all of them together and the
# site starts refusing requests. Verify after deploying with:
#   curl -H "X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4" https://yourdomain/api/config -v
# The server should log your real IP, never 1.2.3.4.
TRUST_PROXY=
